  "body": "As I understand currently:\n\n``` js\nvar stream = new ReadableStream(function (_, _, error) {\n  error(new Error(\"NEVER CAUGHT\")\n})\n\n// do not call `stream.read()`\n```\n\nwill hide the error which is a problem of how promises work.\n\nThis means that any stream that is paused, i.e. right now I do want to read from it can be either a valid Stream or a Stream that is currently in a boxed error state.\n\nnode streams use the push distribution of errors namely `stream.emit(\"error\")` instead of the pull distribution of errors (i.e. do `stream.read()` to check for errors)\n\nThere may be value in having `stream.onError = function () {}` instead of having errors go through the promise. This would mean one consumer has to handle the error and if no-one handles the error then its a thrown exception. Currently with the `.read()` model it doesn't make sense for multiple people to handle the error anyway because there is only one promise reference per read() (assuming that is the case)\n",
